4243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186381153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133789 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1045 "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134003 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8da487d8-5da0-5730-90f5-0cc952b0b118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134243 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r = sv + tw$, where $s, t ∈ R$, and\n$v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6ef098f-928d-59a3-9d4f-bfcb112bab97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134243 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1061 20246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3cce71c-fd0d-5040-949b-813f3fdcbdd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1061 20247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e0f305d-efdf-586e-b799-66fe2b5a8f6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1061 20248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c20a2e38-a98a-579e-9564-09d78a7f10c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1061 20249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "184ad4b9-e662-5d0f-9c92-14f6b77fcc1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134678 #edb/ref 1061 20250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b14c806-2358-52c9-aabe-db4086c3d1ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1064 "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n![](images/28/28ce7470231775d8889555d0dd9f37ee9215044762a40335bd43578b121486a3.svg)\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n![](images/f1/f1e3c7ae9e966ebcab88901c9e44a5449cb98716f43fc526d306e0af5186923b.svg)\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n![](images/47/472c41e1e509d2db9c41dfa95127382eeba7ab5fbaa4172d680c39335694ef2e.svg)\n\nIf we denote \n![](images/26/2650c1020d413a7c988279cc698ab4b7c4caff427615d49b35ef4e9a42651ff0.svg)\nand ![](images/9b/9b50884bd0af404e71f9d7734e9bc054fc9439aba02780cf2beb5fa0a6ac1f32.svg) then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1065 3626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a9a954f-9338-5c3f-a9db-9b39a1a60c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135049 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1064 "If $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is a set of *linearly independent* vectors, then the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $A$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n,$ that is,\n![](images/25/25a85721a3721ace1a32f6ed457749091452df93053d2eba7dbfaa48f6f74189.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The fact that $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ must be linearly independent is important. If the vectors are linearly dependent, we need to find the basis of the span before applying the formula." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1065 3627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3274de58-5861-55c3-9614-08540ad4231e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135050 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Formula for the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1064 "As we've already seen, the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\, \\mathbf{x} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\: \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nIf we suppose that $\\mathbf{a}$ denotes the column-vector ![](images/c2/c23a26d5ce1137e222e28a4bc872f9bcffda2cd6430f2d5532618ccf0ed95fa5.svg) then the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ is simply $\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a},$ and we can write the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{(a\\cdot x)}{(a\\cdot a)}\\,a \\\\  & =[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}]a \\\\  & =a[\\frac{({a}^{T}x)}{({a}^{T}a)}] \\\\  & =a[({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x] \\\\  & =a\\,({a}^{T}a)^{-1}{a}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compare the above with the formula that we use when we want to project onto an arbitrary subspace:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \nAs we can see, they are basically the same thing!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1065 3628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "491ffb4a-98e5-59b5-8c01-3d23f872cc3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135051 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Intuition Behind the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388967 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd7403df-a496-59c6-8226-c07a3e6dd8d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388967 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186388972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388968 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa6cde22-2f73-519a-945c-bffdd259e21b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388969 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]